<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is a clothing manufacturer the same as a factory?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes — \"clothing manufacturer\" and \"garment factory\" are generally used interchangeably. Both terms refer to a facility that physically produces the garments, as opposed to a company that designs, markets, or resells them."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can a clothing manufacturer help design my product too?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Full-package manufacturers often can, through tech pack development and pattern-making support. CMT-only manufacturers can't — they need a finished pattern and fabric supplied to them before they start."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do all clothing manufacturers have a minimum order quantity?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Nearly all of them, yes — it's how a factory keeps per-unit setup costs (cutting, sampling) manageable. MOQs vary widely by manufacturer and garment complexity."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How is a clothing manufacturer different from a clothing brand?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A brand owns the design, marketing, and customer relationship but frequently doesn't own any production — it hires a manufacturer to make the garments. A manufacturer owns the factory but usually isn't the one selling to the end customer."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How many clothing manufacturers are in Bangladesh?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Bangladesh has approximately 4,000 active garment factories according to BGMEA. Of these, 500+ are export-ready factories with international certifications. The industry employs 4 million workers and produces 40% of Bangladesh's total exports. SDF Clothing is one of 500+ certified factories serving international brands."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the minimum order quantity (MOQ) for Bangladesh clothing manufacturers?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "MOQs vary by factory type. Large export factories typically require 1,000-5,000 pieces per style. Mid-sized factories accept 500-1,000 pieces. SDF Clothing offers 300-piece MOQ for knitwear, making us accessible to startup brands. Always verify MOQ requirements before committing to a manufacturer."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What certifications should Bangladesh clothing manufacturers have?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Essential certifications include GOTS 7.0 for organic cotton, OEKO-TEX Standard 100 for chemical safety, BSCI or SEDEX for social compliance, and WRAP for ethical manufacturing. For EU markets, ensure factories are preparing for Digital Product Passport requirements. SDF Clothing holds GOTS 7.0, OEKO-TEX, and BSCI certifications."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How long does production take in Bangladesh clothing manufacturers?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Lead times typically range from 20-60 days depending on complexity. Simple knitwear takes 20-30 days. Complex woven garments require 45-60 days. Peak seasons (July-October for holiday orders) add 15-20 days. SDF Clothing maintains 20-45 day lead times for knitwear with consistent on-time delivery."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is Bangladesh clothing manufacturing cost-effective?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Bangladesh offers 60-70% lower labor costs than China according to McKinsey research. Duty-free access to EU and UK markets through GSP provides additional cost advantages. However, certification compliance adds 15-25% to production costs. Bangladesh remains competitive for certified sustainable manufacturing."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What changed in Bangladesh clothing manufacturing in 2026?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Key 2026 changes include Bangladesh LDC graduation affecting duty structures, increased GOTS 7.0 adoption for EU Digital Product Passport preparation, UFLPA cotton tracing requirements, and CBAM carbon costs affecting coal-powered factories. Manufacturers investing in traceability infrastructure are positioning for 2027 regulations."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I verify Bangladesh clothing manufacturer certifications?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Verify certifications through official databases: GOTS at global-standard.org/database, OEKO-TEX at oeko-tex.com, BSCI through amfori.org, WRAP at wrapcompliance.org. Always request certificate numbers and check expiration dates. SDF Clothing provides verifiable certificate numbers for all our certifications."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What types of clothing do Bangladesh manufacturers produce?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Bangladesh specializes in knitwear (t-shirts, hoodies, activewear), denim jeans, woven shirts, and sweaters. The country produces 40% of EU denim imports. SDF Clothing specializes in knitwear with 300-piece MOQ, making us ideal for startup brands launching first collections."
      }
    }
  ]
}
</script>

