<!-- What Is a Clothing Manufacturer -->
<section id="what-is-a-clothing-manufacturer" style="padding:4rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,3vw,2.4rem);font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.25rem;">What Is a Clothing Manufacturer?</h2>
    <p style="color:#FFFFFF;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:2rem;max-width:800px;">
      A clothing manufacturer is a factory that turns fabric into finished garments — cutting, sewing, and finishing apparel at scale for fashion brands, retailers, and independent designers. A manufacturer is different from a fashion <em>brand</em> (which designs and sells under its own name but may not own any production), a <em>trading company</em> (which resells access to a factory without owning one), and a <em>wholesaler</em> (which sells existing finished stock rather than producing to your specification). If you're trying to tell these apart in practice, see our <a href="/direct-factory-vs-trading-company-vs-buying-agent" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">direct factory vs trading company vs buying agent</a> comparison.
    </p>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Types of Clothing Manufacturers</h3>
    <p style="color:#FFFFFF;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1.5rem;max-width:800px;">Manufacturers are usually described by how much of the process they handle:</p>
    <ul style="color:#FFFFFF;line-height:2;margin-bottom:2rem;max-width:800px;padding-left:1.2rem;">
      <li><strong>Full-package (FPP):</strong> handles fabric sourcing, trims, labels, and packaging in addition to production — see <a href="/full-package-production/" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">full package production</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>CMT (Cut, Make, Trim):</strong> only cuts and sews fabric the buyer supplies — see <a href="/cut-and-sew-service" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">cut and sew service</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>OEM (Original Equipment Manufacturer):</strong> produces to your exact design and tech pack — see <a href="/oem-clothing-manufacturer" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">OEM clothing manufacturer</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Private label:</strong> produces existing designs under your brand name — see <a href="/private-label-clothing-manufacturer" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">private label manufacturer</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="color:#888;font-size:0.85rem;margin-bottom:2rem;">For a full side-by-side breakdown of OEM vs ODM vs CMT, see our <a href="/insights/oem-vs-odm-vs-cmt" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">dedicated guide</a>.</p>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.25rem;">How the Manufacturing Process Works</h3>
    <p style="color:#FFFFFF;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1rem;max-width:800px;">Most orders move through the same broad stages, whichever manufacturer you use:</p>
    <ol style="color:#FFFFFF;line-height:2;margin-bottom:2rem;max-width:800px;padding-left:1.2rem;">
      <li>Design and <a href="/tech-pack-service" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">tech pack</a> development</li>
      <li>Supplier search and quote (see: <a href="/insights/MOQ-meaning-clothing" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">what MOQ actually means</a>)</li>
      <li><a href="/sampling-service" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">Sampling</a> and revisions</li>
      <li>Bulk production</li>
      <li><a href="/quality-inspection-service" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">Quality inspection</a></li>
      <li>Shipping and export documentation</li>
    </ol>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Quick Questions</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #1a1a1a;max-width:800px;">
      <details style="border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
        <summary style="padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;cursor:pointer;color:#fff;font-size:0.9rem;list-style:none;">Is a clothing manufacturer the same as a factory?</summary>
        <p style="padding:0 1.25rem 1.25rem;color:#bbb;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Yes — "clothing manufacturer" and "garment factory" are generally used interchangeably. Both terms refer to a facility that physically produces the garments, as opposed to a company that designs, markets, or resells them.</p>
      </details>
      <details style="border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
        <summary style="padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;cursor:pointer;color:#fff;font-size:0.9rem;list-style:none;">Can a clothing manufacturer help design my product too?</summary>
        <p style="padding:0 1.25rem 1.25rem;color:#bbb;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Full-package manufacturers often can, through tech pack development and pattern-making support. CMT-only manufacturers can't — they need a finished pattern and fabric supplied to them before they start.</p>
      </details>
      <details style="border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
        <summary style="padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;cursor:pointer;color:#fff;font-size:0.9rem;list-style:none;">Do all clothing manufacturers have a minimum order quantity?</summary>
        <p style="padding:0 1.25rem 1.25rem;color:#bbb;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Nearly all of them, yes — it's how a factory keeps per-unit setup costs (cutting, sampling) manageable. MOQs vary widely by manufacturer and garment complexity; see our <a href="/insights/MOQ-meaning-clothing" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">MOQ guide</a> for how it's typically calculated.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary style="padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;cursor:pointer;color:#fff;font-size:0.9rem;list-style:none;">How is a clothing manufacturer different from a clothing brand?</summary>
        <p style="padding:0 1.25rem 1.25rem;color:#bbb;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">A brand owns the design, marketing, and customer relationship but frequently doesn't own any production — it hires a manufacturer to make the garments. A manufacturer owns the factory but usually isn't the one selling to the end customer.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Why This Category Matters -->
<section id="why-category-matters" style="padding:4rem 0;">
